sfies split)\n- client prog: connect, send GET, recv(750000), reset  ← real download\n- server prog: recv(len(req)), reset  ← dummy (real server responds)\n- cps=500, cont=True, mult=1, server_pool .3-.5\n\nWait but server template must send something for client recv to proceed IF both emulated. But we established real server responds (verify). So dummy server template recv+reset should let real server data flow to client recv. \n\nBut with server pool .3-.5 and cont, connections to .4/.5: SYN to .4/.5, no response (blackhole), connect hangs. So only .3 connections do real download. 1/3 efficiency.\n\nUgh the 1/3. Alternatively use server pool .3-.3.255? No worse.\n\nLet me reconsider: what if I make the server pool exactly [.3, .3, .3]? ip_range needs [start,end]. [.3,.3] = 1 address. Can't have 3 identical via range.\n\nWhat if I use MULTIPLE templates, each a single-shot real download to .3, and rely on mult? With cps default 1 per template... \n\nOK let me just take the pragmatic combined approach like the FINAL script but refined, OR test pure ASTF. Given uncertainty, let me do a DIAGNOSTIC run first that builds the real-download ASTF profile with server pool .3-.5, cps modest, and reports whether load_profile succeeds and whether tx/rx flows. This is iteration 1 (real). Then iterate based on results.\n\nActually, let me reconsider time budget. I'll make iteration 1 a solid attempt with the real-server download flood using server pool .3-.5, cont, cps, and ALSO include a handful of Python real-download threads as a reliable large-file-download component. This way:\n- If ASTF works → combined heavy load\n- If ASTF partially works → python threads still pull large files\n- I get real measurement of RTT/loss\n\nThis matches the proven \"FINAL\" reference approach. Let me refine it:\n- ASTF real download: client connect, send, recv(750000), reset; server recv, reset; server pool .3-.5; cps=1000, mult=20, cont=True\n- Python threads: 50 threads each looping: GET the